tzint - a Tcl package for the libzint barcode library
=====================================================

tzint encodes barcodes from Tcl. It embeds the libzint backend by Robin Stuart
and exposes it as a single command, ::tzint::Encode, that hands the result back
in a Tcl variable or writes it to a file. No Tk is needed; the package produces
data, not windows.

Over 80 symbologies are supported, among them Code 128, Code 39, EAN and UPC,
ITF, QR Code, Micro QR, Data Matrix, PDF417, MicroPDF417, Aztec Code, MaxiCode,
GS1 DataBar, Code 16k, Code One, Grid Matrix, the postal codes of several
countries and the composite symbologies. The command "::tzint::Encode
symbologies" lists the names this build accepts.

This package is built with the Tcl Extension Architecture (TEA); see
http://www.tcl.tk/doc/tea/ for what that means for the build system.


REQUIREMENTS
============

Tcl 8.5 or later, including the 9.x series. The package uses the stubs
mechanism, so a build works with any Tcl of that range.

libpng and zlib, for the PNG output. Both are looked for by the configure
script; see BUILDING below for the options that point it at them.

A tclConfig.sh from the Tcl installation you want to build against. TEA takes
the compiler and its flags from there.


BUILDING
========

Unix, Linux and macOS:

	$ ./configure --with-tcl=/path/to/lib
	$ make
	$ make install

--with-tcl names the directory holding tclConfig.sh. If the PNG headers or
libraries are not in the default location, point the configure script at them:

	--with-png-include=DIR	directory holding png.h
	--with-png-lib=DIR	directory holding the libpng library
	--enable-png-static	link libpng statically

The configure script is generated from configure.ac and tclconfig/tcl.m4; after
changing either, regenerate it with autoconf.

Note that the build writes its object files into the source directory. A build
tree separate from the source directory therefore needs a "make clean" in the
source directory first, otherwise make finds the stale objects through VPATH
and links them instead of compiling new ones.

Windows:

The Msys and Mingw route uses the configure script above and produces native
binaries. For a build with Visual C++ use the files in the win subdirectory:

	> nmake -f makefile.vc
	> nmake -f makefile.vc install INSTALLDIR=c:\path\to\tcl

The object list in win/makefile.vc is maintained by hand and has to match the
sources listed in configure.ac.


INSTALLING
==========

"make install" puts the shared library and pkgIndex.tcl into a versioned
directory below the Tcl installation, so that

	package require tzint

finds it. On Windows any dependent DLLs belong in a directory on PATH.


USING
=====

	package require tzint

	# the version of the embedded libzint, and what it can encode
	::tzint::Encode version
	::tzint::Encode symbologies

	# a matrix of "0" and "1" rows, one string per pixel row
	::tzint::Encode bits data "Hello Tcl" -barcode code128
	dict get $data width
	dict get $data height
	foreach row [dict get $data bits] { puts $row }

	# an X bitmap, ready for "image create bitmap -data ..."
	::tzint::Encode xbm data "Hello Tcl" -barcode qrcode

	# vector output, as a string
	::tzint::Encode svg data "Hello Tcl" -barcode qrcode
	::tzint::Encode eps data "Hello Tcl" -barcode code128

	# straight to a file, the extension picks the format
	::tzint::Encode file symbol.png "Hello Tcl" -barcode code128

Encode returns 0 when libzint reports no problem, and a non-zero status
otherwise. The -stat option collects the details:

	::tzint::Encode bits data "Hello Tcl" -barcode code128 -stat info
	# info holds: zint, error, rows, columns, width, height

Options that tzint itself rejects, such as an unknown symbology or a value out
of range, raise a Tcl error instead.

Two further commands describe the build of the package itself:

	::tzint::manifest	a dictionary with version, date, check-in,
				build-hash and uuid
	::tzint::build-info	compiler and build details, in the manner of
				::tcl::build-info. Only present when the
				interpreter provides that command, from Tcl 8.7 on.

The file command is described in doc/tzint.unsupported rather than in the
manual page.


TESTING
=======

	$ make test

runs the tcltest suite in the tests directory against the library just built.


DOCUMENTATION
=============

doc/tzint.n		manual page, describing every command and option
doc/tzintsample.tcl	a runnable example
doc/tzint.unsupported	the parts deliberately kept out of the manual page

The libzint manual documents the symbologies themselves and the meaning of
their options:

	https://zint.org.uk/manual/


CONTENTS
========

generic/	the tzint sources and the embedded libzint backend
doc/		manual page and example
tests/		the tcltest suite
tools/		mkversion.tcl, which derives manifest.h from the checkout
win/		makefile.vc and friends for the Visual C++ build
tclconfig/	tcl.m4 and install-sh, taken from the TEA project


